q('P','Cloning','structuredClone cycles','What does cloning this cyclic record log?','true true','structuredClone creates a new root object.~It preserves the graph’s cycle, so self refers to the cloned root. JSON serialization would reject this cycle.','Think in graphs.','Where should the copied self reference point?','mdn:Web/API/Window/structuredClone','const original = { id: 1 };\noriginal.self = original;\nconst copy = structuredClone(original);\nconsole.log(copy !== original, copy.self === copy);')
q('M','Cloning','Uncloneable values','Which value causes structuredClone to throw DataCloneError?','An ordinary function.','Functions are not supported by structured cloning. Dates, Maps and cyclic plain objects are supported. A clone is not an arbitrary executable-object copier.','Separate data from executable behavior.','Which value contains callable code?','mdn:Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Structured_clone_algorithm',wrong=['A Date instance.','A Map with string keys and number values.','A plain object referencing itself.'])
q('P','Serialization','JSON omissions','What exact JSON string is logged?','{"count":2}','JSON.stringify omits object properties whose values are undefined.~The numeric count remains. In arrays, unsupported undefined entries become null instead of disappearing.','Object and array serialization differ.','Which object value is omitted?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify','console.log(JSON.stringify({ count: 2, note: undefined }));')
q('P','Equality','Object.is distinctions','What do these comparisons log?','true false','Object.is considers NaN the same value as NaN.~It distinguishes positive and negative zero, unlike ===. Neither comparison recursively compares object fields.','This is SameValue comparison.','There are two differences from ===.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/is','console.log(Object.is(NaN, NaN), Object.is(0, -0));')
q('P','Arrays','Immutable sorting','What does the nonmutating sort log?','1,2,3\n3,1,2','toSorted builds a sorted copy using the comparator.~The source array keeps its original order. Object entries themselves are still shared.','Check the method prefix.','Which array receives the ordering?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/toSorted','const values = [3, 1, 2];\nconst ordered = values.toSorted((a, b) => a - b);\nconsole.log(ordered.join(","));\nconsole.log(values.join(","));')
q('W','Arrays','Grouping records','Implement groupByTeam(records) using standardized Object.groupBy. Each record has a string team.','function groupByTeam(records) {\n  return Object.groupBy(records, record => record.team);\n}\n// Time: O(n). Output storage: O(n).','The callback selects a key for each record.~Records with equal keys enter the same array.~The result has a null prototype; records are not cloned, and missing groups are absent.','Use the key-producing callback.','Which grouping method returns an object?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/groupBy','// Input example: [{ team: "web", name: "Ada" }]\n// Implement groupByTeam(records).')
q('P','Arrays','flatMap expansion','What does this tag expansion log?','a,b,c','flatMap maps each string to an array.~It flattens one level, producing three strings. It does not recursively flatten arbitrary nested arrays.','Map first, then flatten once.','What does each split return?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/flatMap','const fields = ["a,b", "c"];\nconsole.log(fields.flatMap(field => field.split(",")).join(","));')
q('P','Arrays','Negative indexing','What does this history lookup log?','last','at(-1) counts one position backward from length.~Bracket access with -1 would look for a property named "-1". Empty arrays return undefined here.','Negative indexing is method-specific.','Where does -1 start counting?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/at','const history = ["first", "last"];\nconsole.log(history.at(-1));')
q('P','Arrays','Sparse array callbacks','How many callbacks run for map?','1','Only index 1 exists in the sparse array.~map skips holes and calls the callback once. An explicitly stored undefined entry would be visited.','A hole is not an own entry.','Which indexes exist?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map#using_map_on_sparse_arrays','const values = new Array(3);\nvalues[1] = 5;\nlet calls = 0;\nvalues.map(value => {\n  calls++;\n  return value;\n});\nconsole.log(calls);')
q('B','Arrays','fill shared reference','Why do all rows change, and how do you create three independent rows?','fill reuses one object. Use: const rows = Array.from({ length: 3 }, () => ({ count: 0 }));','The object literal is evaluated once before fill.~Every slot receives that reference.~A mapping factory evaluates a new literal per entry.','Count object allocations.','Does fill call a factory?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/fill','const rows = Array(3).fill({ count: 0 });\nrows[0].count = 1;')
q('C','Modules','ESM versus CommonJS','What matters when publishing a browser library as ESM rather than CommonJS?','ESM uses standardized import/export declarations and live bindings. CommonJS uses a host-specific module.exports/require system. Browser-native modules use ESM; CommonJS needs a compatible tool or runtime.','Static ESM structure supports module linking and analysis. Interoperability details depend on the host and bundler, so treating all default imports as identical is unsafe.','Separate language modules from host conventions.','Does the browser expose require?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules')
q('S','Modules','Live exported bindings','A module exports let count and an increment function. A consumer imports count. Does calling increment update what the consumer reads?','Yes, the import observes the exported binding. It is not a snapshot copied during import. The consumer cannot assign directly to that imported binding.','The exporting module can update its own mutable binding. Copying the imported value into a new local variable creates a separate value snapshot.','Imports have binding semantics.','Who owns assignment permission?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/import')
q('M','Modules','Dynamic import','What does import("./editor.js") return?','A promise that fulfills with the module namespace object.','Dynamic import is asynchronous and can fail. It returns a namespace object through a promise, not the default export directly.','Consider loading completion.','Where would the default export be found?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/import',wrong=['The default export synchronously.','A CommonJS require function.','An HTML script element.'])
q('C','Modules','Module isolation','Why does a top-level variable in a browser module not become window.someName?','Modules have their own scope. Top-level bindings do not become properties of the global object. Explicitly exporting a binding makes it available to other modules.','Module scope differs from classic-script var behavior. Modules also run in strict mode, but strict mode alone is not an export mechanism.','Identify the scope boundary.','Does declaring mean exporting?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules#other_differences_between_modules_and_classic_scripts')
q('P','Iterators','Generator suspension','What does this generator log?','start\n1\nresume\n2','Calling sequence creates an iterator without running its body.~The first next runs to the first yield.~The second resumes after that yield and reaches the next.','Creation is not execution.','Each next resumes a suspended body.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/function*','function* sequence() {\n  console.log("start");\n  yield 1;\n  console.log("resume");\n  yield 2;\n}\nconst iterator = sequence();\nconsole.log(iterator.next().value);\nconsole.log(iterator.next().value);')
q('P','Iterators','Generator return','What does spreading this generator log?','1,2','Spread consumes yielded values until done is true.~The final return value accompanies done:true and is not included. Direct next calls could inspect it.','A return is not a yield.','What does for-of consume?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/function*#return_statement_in_a_generator','function* values() {\n  yield 1;\n  yield 2;\n  return 3;\n}\nconsole.log([...values()].join(","));')
q('W','Iterators','Finite range iterable','Implement a generator range(start, end) yielding integers from start inclusive to end exclusive. Inputs are integers.','function* range(start, end) {\n  for (let value = start; value < end; value++) {\n    yield value;\n  }\n}\n// Time: O(max(0, end - start)). Auxiliary space: O(1).','Iteration resumes for each requested value.~The loop stops before end.~If start is at least end, it yields nothing; collecting results adds output storage.','Use lazy production.','Suspend once per integer.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/function*','// Implement range(start, end).\n// Example consumption: [...range(2, 5)]')
q('M','Iterators','Iterable protocol','Which member makes an ordinary object consumable by for...of?','A Symbol.iterator method returning an iterator.','for...of requests an iterator through Symbol.iterator. A length property alone creates neither an iterator nor iteration behavior. next returns value/done records.','Find the protocol entry point.','Which well-known symbol is consulted?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Iteration_protocols',wrong=['A numeric length property.','A toString method.','A property named iterator containing an array.'])
q('T','Iterators','Generator input','Trace the values passed into this generator.','ready\n12','The first next starts the generator and yields ready.~The argument to the second next becomes the suspended yield expression’s result.~The generator yields twice that value. The first next argument would be ignored.','next can send as well as receive.','Which yield receives the argument?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Generator/next','function* calculate() {\n  const value = yield "ready";\n  yield value * 2;\n}\nconst iterator = calculate();\nconsole.log(iterator.next().value);\nconsole.log(iterator.next(6).value);')
q('P','Functions','Currying','What does the curried pricing function log?','12','add receives 5 and returns a closure retaining it.~Calling that closure with 7 sums the two arguments. Currying changes the calling convention, not numeric addition.','Track one argument per call.','What does the first call return?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Closures','const add = first => second => first + second;\nconst addFee = add(5);\nconsole.log(addFee(7));')
q('W','Functions','Partial application','Write withPrefix(prefix) returning a function that prefixes a supplied string with prefix and a colon.','function withPrefix(prefix) {\n  return value => `${prefix}:${value}`;\n}\n// Time and output space: O(prefix.length + value.length).','The outer call captures prefix.~The returned function formats each value using that binding.~Separate outer calls preserve independent prefixes.','Keep one argument in a closure.','Return a function for the remaining input.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Closures','// withPrefix("user")("42") must return "user:42".')
q('C','Rate limiting','Debounce and throttle','For a search box and a continuous drag indicator, how do debounce and throttle differ?','A trailing debounce waits for a quiet period before running. A throttle limits how often work runs during continued activity. Choose edge behavior explicitly so the final value is not accidentally lost.','Resetting a timer implements quiet-period behavior, not fixed-frequency execution. Timer delays are minimum scheduling delays, not real-time guarantees.','Compare quiet time and ongoing activity.','Must updates happen while events continue?','mdn:Glossary/Debounce')
q('W','Rate limiting','Trailing debounce','Implement a trailing debounce(fn, delay) preserving this and the latest arguments. No cancel or leading option is required.','function debounce(fn, delay) {\n  let timer;\n  return function (...args) {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);\n  };\n}\n// O(1) scheduling per call; O(k) retained arguments.','Each call cancels the previous pending timer.~The new arrow retains this and the latest arguments.~Only the final timer invokes fn after a quiet interval; actual execution can be delayed by a busy event loop.','Retain a timer handle.','Which callback should survive a burst?','mdn:Web/API/Window/setTimeout','// Implement debounce(fn, delay).\n// fn is synchronous; delay is a nonnegative number.')
q('U','Rate limiting','Debounce cleanup','Follow-up to JS-073: why should a UI component be able to cancel its debounced callback when it unmounts?','The callback may retain component resources until its timer fires. It can also perform work after the component is no longer relevant. A cancel operation should clear the pending timer and release retained references when appropriate.','Removing an event listener prevents new scheduling but does not cancel an already scheduled timeout. These are separate cleanup obligations.','Think about already scheduled work.','Does removing a listener clear its timers?','mdn:Web/API/Window/clearTimeout')
q('P','Objects','Object.entries transformation','What does this configuration transformation log?','{"a":2,"b":4}','Object.entries creates key/value pairs for enumerable own string keys.~map doubles each value.~fromEntries constructs a new object. Symbol keys would not be included by entries.','Follow the pair pipeline.','Which keys does entries expose?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/fromEntries','const source = { a: 1, b: 2 };\nconst doubled = Object.fromEntries(\n  Object.entries(source).map(([key, value]) => [key, value * 2])\n);\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(doubled));')
q('P','Objects','Computed keys','What does this property update log?','3','The computed property name evaluates field to "count".~The later definition overwrites the earlier count property. The field identifier itself is not used as the key.','Brackets evaluate an expression.','Which key string results?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Object_initializer#computed_property_names','const field = "count";\nconst state = { count: 1, [field]: 3 };\nconsole.log(state.count);')
q('B','Control flow','Automatic semicolon insertion','Why does make return undefined, and how should its return statement be written?','A line break after return ends the statement. Use: return { ok: true };','The parser inserts a semicolon after return.~The following braces are not the returned object expression.~Keeping the expression on the return line avoids the restricted-production boundary.','Inspect the newline after return.','Can a return expression begin on the next line?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/return#automatic_semicolon_insertion','function make() {\n  return\n  { ok: true };\n}')
q('P','Errors','finally completion','What does this function log?','cleanup\nvalue','The return value is prepared in try.~finally executes before the function completes.~Because finally does not return or throw, the original return is preserved.','A return still runs finally.','Does cleanup replace the completion?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/try...catch#the_finally_block','function read() {\n  try {\n    return "value";\n  } finally {\n    console.log("cleanup");\n  }\n}\nconsole.log(read());')
q('C','Errors','Error cause','Why wrap a low-level error with Error(message, { cause }) at an API boundary?','It adds operation-specific context. The original failure remains available through cause. Callers can inspect or report both levels without relying on one flattened message.','Replacing the error with a string loses structured information. Error causes are not automatically safe to show to end users, because lower layers can contain sensitive details.','Preserve diagnostic context.','Can the original error remain attached?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/cause')
q('P','Numbers','BigInt arithmetic','What does this integer calculation log?','9007199254740994','Both operands are BigInts, so addition preserves the integer exactly.~String converts the result to decimal text without an n suffix. Mixing a Number operand into this addition would throw.','Check operand types.','The n suffix selects a different numeric type.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/BigInt','const id = 9007199254740993n;\nconsole.log(String(id + 1n));')
q('M','Strings','Unicode iteration','For a single non-BMP emoji, which operation counts Unicode code points rather than UTF-16 code units?','[...text].length','String iteration yields code points. length counts UTF-16 code units, while split("") also splits code units. Code points still do not always equal user-perceived graphemes.','There are several notions of character.','Which operation uses the string iterator?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/Symbol.iterator',wrong=['text.length','text.split("").length','text.length / 2'])
q('W','Strings','Grapheme segmentation','Implement countCharacters(text) counting user-perceived grapheme clusters with Intl.Segmenter.','function countCharacters(text) {\n  const segmenter = new Intl.Segmenter(undefined, {\n    granularity: "grapheme"\n  });\n  return Array.from(segmenter.segment(text)).length;\n}\n// O(n) traversal and O(n) collected segments.','Create a grapheme segmenter.~Segment the input and count yielded segments.~Combined marks and joined emoji can form one cluster; code-point counting can overcount them.','Use segmentation, not code-unit length.','Which granularity groups visible characters?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/Segmenter','// Implement countCharacters(text).\n// Treat combined emoji sequences as grapheme clusters.')
q('B','Regular expressions','Stateful global test','Why can repeated validation alternate results, and what replacement avoids stateful validation?','The global regex updates lastIndex. Use: const valid = /^\d+$/;','test with g begins at lastIndex and advances it on success.~A later test may start after the input and fail.~Remove g for whole-string validation; reset lastIndex only when deliberately reusing stateful iteration.','Inspect regex flags.','Does this regex retain a cursor?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/RegExp/test','const valid = /^\d+$/g;\nvalid.test("123");\nvalid.test("123");')
q('P','Regular expressions','Capture extraction','What does the named capture log?','42','The pattern matches the full user identifier.~The named group captures its digits.~groups.id contains text, not a number; a failed match would return null.','Capture groups preserve substrings.','Which part is named id?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Regular_expressions/Named_capturing_group','const match = /^user-(?<id>\d+)$/.exec("user-42");\nconsole.log(match.groups.id);')
q('C','Collections','Map key equality','How does Map compare keys, including object keys and NaN?','It uses SameValueZero. NaN matches NaN and signed zeros match each other. Object keys match only by identity.','A structurally equal object is still a different key. Serializing object keys changes identity semantics and can introduce collisions.','Identify the equality algorithm.','Are objects compared recursively?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map#key_equality')
q('P','Collections','Map update ordering','What order is logged after updating an existing Map key?','a,b','Setting an existing key changes its value.~It does not move the key to the end of insertion order. Deleting and reinserting would move it.','Update differs from reinsertion.','Was a new entry created?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map/set','const cache = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2]]);\ncache.set("a", 3);\nconsole.log([...cache.keys()].join(","));')
q('S','Collections','WeakMap metadata','You attach internal metadata to DOM elements. Why might WeakMap be preferable to adding properties to each element?','WeakMap keeps metadata external to the element. Object keys do not stay alive merely because they are keys. Its entries cannot be enumerated, so choose another structure if listing all keys is required.','WeakMap prevents this mapping from being the retaining root. Other references can still retain the element; weak storage does not guarantee immediate collection.','Think about ownership and reachability.','Do you need to enumerate the metadata?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/WeakMap')
q('P','Immutability','Shallow freeze','What does this nested update log?','2','freeze affects the root’s own property descriptors.~The nested object remains mutable and is updated.~Deep immutability requires handling the graph, not freezing only one object.','Which object was frozen?','Does freeze recurse?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/freeze','"use strict";\nconst state = Object.freeze({ nested: { count: 1 } });\nstate.nested.count = 2;\nconsole.log(state.nested.count);')
q('M','Objects','Seal versus freeze','Which operation prevents adding/removing own properties while allowing writes to existing writable data properties?','Object.seal(object)','seal makes own properties non-configurable and prevents extension. It does not make writable data properties read-only. freeze adds that restriction.','Separate shape from values.','Which operation locks only the shape and configurability?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/seal',wrong=['Object.freeze(object)','Object.assign({}, object)','Object.keys(object)'])
q('W','Functions','once wrapper','Implement once(fn) for a synchronous function: cache the first successful return; if it throws, allow a later retry. Preserve this and arguments.','function once(fn) {\n  let done = false;\n  let value;\n  return function (...args) {\n    if (!done) {\n      value = fn.apply(this, args);\n      done = true;\n    }\n    return value;\n  };\n}\n// Wrapper overhead: O(1) time and retained state.','Call fn only while done is false.~Store its value before marking completion.~If fn throws, done stays false. A successful undefined result is still cached.','Track success separately from the result.','Could undefined be a valid result?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Closures','// Implement once(fn).\n// fn is synchronous and does not recursively call the wrapper.')
q('T','Functions','Recursive call stack','Trace this recursive total and give the console output.','6','sum(3) waits for sum(2), which waits for sum(1).~sum(0) returns the base value zero.~The stack unwinds to 1, 3 and 6. Deep inputs risk stack overflow.','Find the base case.','Add values while unwinding.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Functions#recursion','function sum(n) {\n  return n === 0 ? 0 : n + sum(n - 1);\n}\nconsole.log(sum(3));')
q('W','Arrays','Linear search index','Implement indexById(items, id) returning the first matching index or -1. Do not sort the input.','function indexById(items, id) {\n  return items.findIndex(item => item.id === id);\n}\n// Worst-case time: O(n). Auxiliary space: O(1).','Visit records in order.~Stop when the predicate succeeds.~Return -1 for no match, including empty input. Index zero is a valid successful result.','The result is an index.','Which method stops at the first predicate match?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/findIndex','// Implement indexById(items, id).\n// Compare ids using strict equality.')
q('B','Arrays','Mutation during iteration','Why can this loop skip adjacent completed tasks, and what replacement safely removes them?','splice shifts later entries before the iterator advances. Use: const remaining = tasks.filter(task => !task.done);','Removing index i shifts the next item into i.~The iteration advances past that shifted item.~filter produces a new array without changing the array being traversed.','Watch shifting indexes.','Can selection avoid in-place deletion?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/forEach#description','tasks.forEach((task, index) => {\n  if (task.done) tasks.splice(index, 1);\n});')
q('P','Objects','Object rest exclusion','What JSON does this sanitized projection log?','{"name":"Ada"}','Destructuring reads password into a separate binding.~Rest collects the remaining enumerable own properties.~This is a shallow projection, not recursive secret removal.','Rest excludes already selected keys.','What remains after password is selected?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Destructuring#rest_properties_and_rest_elements','const user = { name: "Ada", password: "secret" };\nconst { password, ...publicUser } = user;\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(publicUser));')
q('M','Symbols','Unique symbols','Two plugins call Symbol("cache") separately. How do the resulting symbols compare?','They are distinct even though their descriptions match.','Symbol creates a new unique symbol each time. Descriptions help debugging but are not identity keys; Symbol.for uses a shared registry instead.','Description is not identity.','Which factory consults a registry?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Symbol',wrong=['They are strictly equal because descriptions match.','They are equal only in strict mode.','They both become the string "cache" as property keys.'])
q('P','Symbols','Symbol-key enumeration','What does ordinary key enumeration log?','name\n2','Object.keys includes enumerable own string keys only.~Reflect.ownKeys also includes the symbol key.~Non-enumerable own keys would also appear in Reflect.ownKeys.','Enumeration APIs expose different key sets.','Which API includes symbols?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Reflect/ownKeys','const hidden = Symbol("id");\nconst user = { name: "Ada", [hidden]: 42 };\nconsole.log(Object.keys(user).join(","));\nconsole.log(Reflect.ownKeys(user).length);')
q('C','Dates','Explicit time zones','Why should an API timestamp include Z or an explicit UTC offset?','It makes the represented instant unambiguous. A date-time without an offset may be interpreted in local time. Display formatting should choose a user-facing time zone separately.','A Date stores an instant, not the original named time zone. Parsing ambiguous locale-specific strings makes cross-environment behavior unreliable.','Separate an instant from local calendar text.','What offset should parsing use?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Date/parse')
q('W','Numbers','Locale formatting','Implement formatAmount(amount, locale, currency) using Intl.NumberFormat rather than concatenating a currency symbol.','function formatAmount(amount, locale, currency) {\n  return new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {\n    style: "currency",\n    currency\n  }).format(amount);\n}','Build a formatter with the requested locale and currency.~Format the numeric amount using its currency rules.~Currency fraction digits vary; do not assume every currency uses two decimals.','Use locale-aware formatting.','Which options select currency display?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/NumberFormat','// amount is finite; locale and currency are valid.\n// Implement formatAmount(amount, locale, currency).')
q('S','Modules','Tree shaking limits','Why does converting a package to ESM not guarantee that every unused export disappears from a production bundle?','ESM makes dependency analysis easier. The bundler must still preserve observable side effects. Package metadata, import patterns and build configuration affect what can be removed.','Static syntax is an enabling condition, not a promise of zero unused code. A module with top-level side effects may still need evaluation.','Consider module evaluation effects.','Can deleting an import change behavior?','mdn:Glossary/Tree_shaking')
q('U','Immutability','Structural sharing','Follow-up to JS-050: why preserve unchanged record identities when updating one list item?','It avoids unnecessary object allocation. It also lets identity-based consumers recognize unchanged records. The optimization is valid only if those shared records are treated immutably.','Deep-cloning every entry destroys useful identity information. Sharing mutable records can instead hide changes from identity comparisons.','Identity can communicate unchanged data.','What assumption makes sharing safe?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax#copying_an_array')
